Add an AV1 backend and advertise it

The H713's AV1 core decodes bit-exactly through GStreamer's v4l2slav1dec; this
makes it reachable from mpv and ffmpeg.  It needs 0014, since the core is a
second decoder device, and it is the first backend on a multi-planar device:
hantro speaks the MPLANE API where cedrus does not, so a device now records
which it speaks, and surface creation picks the OUTPUT type and the capture
format entry to match (NV12 gains a multi-planar entry: one buffer holding both
planes, as hantro allocates it).

VA-API's AV1 picture parameters carry nearly the whole frame header and ffmpeg
sends only tile data, so this is a translation rather than a parser.  The gaps
are what a decoder with its own DPB needs no one to tell it:

  - each reference's order hint, which a surface now remembers from its own
    decode;
  - skip_mode_frame[], derived from those as skip_mode_params() does;
  - tile_size_bytes, recovered from the gap a size field leaves between two
    tiles of one tile group (4 when no tile has one);
  - refresh_frame_flags, sent as 0: the kernel driver finds every reference,
    its CDFs included, by the timestamp of the surface in the slot;
  - the downscaled width under superres, derived from the coded (upscaled)
    width VA-API carries, as superres_params() does.

Two layout details.  Tile groups arrive as one parameter buffer and one data
buffer each, parameters first, so a tile's offset is the group's place in the
bitstream buffer plus its offset within the group.  And four zero bytes go in
front of the first group: with several tiles the kernel points the core at
tile 0's size field, which does not exist when tile 0 is alone in its group.

Film grain: ffmpeg decodes a grained frame into one surface (the one later
frames reference) and shows another, current_display_picture.  The kernel
keeps its references privately and writes only the grained picture, so the
CAPTURE buffer queued is the display surface's, and the sync dequeues that.

Profile 0, 8-bit only: 10-bit sequences are refused by the kernel.

The surface size limit was a flat 2048x2048 for every profile, which kept 4K
AV1 in software.  AV1 now takes the size range its device reports through
VIDIOC_ENUM_FRAMESIZES (4096x2304 on the H713, where 4K decodes bit-exactly).
Every other profile keeps 2048: cedrus claims 4096x2304 for all its formats,
and none of them has been run that large.

The DRM PRIME export described the whole allocation as the picture.  With
cedrus that never mattered -- it rounds heights to 16, and 720 is a multiple --
but hantro rounds to 64, so a 720p AV1 surface exported as 1280x768: mpv's
vo=drm built a 768-line framebuffer, the H713 video plane (which takes exactly
the 1280x720 picture) refused every flip with EINVAL, and the panel showed
nothing while decode ran at full rate.  The descriptor now gives the size the
client asked for, capped by the allocation; pitches and offsets still describe
the memory, and a scaled surface, smaller than asked, is described as before.

After a frame fails, AV1 frames are refused until the next key frame.  They
could only decode to garbage, since they predict from the failure; and ffmpeg
abandons the rest of a temporal unit once one frame in it fails, having
already parsed all of it, so its reference state -- order hints, and every
header field parsed conditionally on them -- no longer matches what was
decoded.  One of the headers it then produced hung the H713 SoC outright on a
damaged test stream; the same stream through GStreamer, which sends every
frame, only makes the core time out.

